 "result": "163 tests ran: 161 passed, 1 failed and 1 errored.\n\n**1. `test_parse_amount` (`test_parse.TestParse`), ERROR**\n```\nFile \"tests/test_parse.py\", line 7, in test_parse_amount\n    self.assertEqual(parse_amount(\"1,234.50\"), 1234.5)\nFile \"calc/parse.py\", line 5, in parse_amount\n    return float(text.strip())\nValueError: could not convert string to float: '1,234.50'\n```\n`parse_amount` hands the string straight to `float()`, which rejects the thousands-separator comma. It raises an exception, so the test never gets as far as checking the result.\n\n**2. `test_discount_rounding` (`test_pricing.TestPricing`), FAIL**\n```\nFile \"tests/test_pricing.py\", line 7, in test_discount_rounding\n    self.assertEqual(apply_discount(2.30, 50), 1.15)\nAssertionError: 1.14 != 1.15\n```\n`apply_discount(2.30, 50)` returned `1.14` where the test expects `1.15`. That points to a float rounding or truncation problem: 2.30 \u00d7 0.5 is stored as roughly 1.1499999\u2026, which comes out as 1.14. I haven't opened `calc/pricing.py` to confirm how it rounds.\n\nAll 160 `test_many` cases and `test_pricing.test_no_discount` passed. I didn't change any files.",
